What is Kubernetes?

Kubernetes (K8s) is a container orchestration platform.
It manages containerized apps (often Docker images) — deployment, scaling,
networking, load balancing, and self-healing.


Main components (learn these first)

  Pod ⭐
  Deployment
  ReplicaSet
  Service
  ConfigMap
  Secret
  Namespace
  Ingress
  Node
  Cluster


═══════════════════════════════════════
1) Pod
═══════════════════════════════════════

Smallest deployable unit in Kubernetes.
Usually 1 Pod = 1 app container (can be more for sidecars).

  Pod
   └── Spring Boot Container

Example with 3 replicas:

  Pod-1 → Spring Boot
  Pod-2 → Spring Boot
  Pod-3 → Spring Boot

Note: Pods are ephemeral — recreate → new IP. That’s why Service exists.


═══════════════════════════════════════
2) Deployment
═══════════════════════════════════════

Manages Pods (desired state).

Does:
  - create Pods
  - keep desired replica count
  - rolling updates
  - rollbacks

  Deployment
      ├── Pod 1
      ├── Pod 2
      └── Pod 3

  replicas: 3  →  Kubernetes tries to keep 3 Pods running


═══════════════════════════════════════
3) ReplicaSet
═══════════════════════════════════════

Ensures the required number of Pod copies are running.

  Deployment
       ↓
  ReplicaSet
       ↓
  Pod  Pod  Pod

You usually do NOT create ReplicaSets by hand — Deployment owns them.


═══════════════════════════════════════
4) Service
═══════════════════════════════════════

Stable network name/IP in front of changing Pods + load-balances traffic.

               Service
             /    |    \
          Pod-1 Pod-2 Pod-3

Common Service types:

  ClusterIP      → internal only (default). e.g. payment-service:8080
  NodePort       → expose on each Node’s port (dev/demo)
  LoadBalancer   → cloud LB in front (AWS/GCP/Azure)

App calls:  http://payment-service:8080
Service selects Pods via labels.


═══════════════════════════════════════
5) ConfigMap
═══════════════════════════════════════

Non-sensitive config (key/value or files).

  DB_HOST=postgres
  APP_PORT=8080

Injected as env vars or mounted files into Pods.


═══════════════════════════════════════
6) Secret
═══════════════════════════════════════

Sensitive data:

  DB_USERNAME
  DB_PASSWORD
  API_KEY

  ConfigMap → normal config
  Secret    → sensitive config

Note: default Secrets are base64-encoded (not strong encryption).
Still better than plain ConfigMap; use KMS/sealed-secrets in real prod.


═══════════════════════════════════════
7) Namespace
═══════════════════════════════════════

Logical isolation inside one cluster (not separate clusters).

  Cluster
   ├── dev
   ├── staging
   └── production

Useful for teams/envs; resource names unique per namespace.


═══════════════════════════════════════
8) Ingress
═══════════════════════════════════════

HTTP/HTTPS entry from outside the cluster (path/host routing + TLS).

  User
   ↓
  Ingress
   ↓
  Service
   ↓
  Pods

Example routes:

  /api/users   → user-service
  /api/orders  → order-service
  api.myapp.com → api-service

Needs an Ingress Controller (e.g. NGINX, Traefik).


═══════════════════════════════════════
9) Node
═══════════════════════════════════════

A worker machine (VM/bare metal) that runs Pods.

  Cluster
   ├── Node 1 → Pods
   ├── Node 2 → Pods
   └── Node 3 → Pods

Worker node agents (short):
  kubelet        → talks to API, runs Pods
  kube-proxy     → Service networking
  container runtime → containerd / CRI-O (runs containers)


═══════════════════════════════════════
10) Cluster
═══════════════════════════════════════

Whole Kubernetes environment = Control Plane + worker Nodes.

  Kubernetes Cluster
  │
  ├── Control Plane (brain)
  │     API Server, Scheduler, Controller Manager, etcd
  │
  ├── Node 1 → Pods
  ├── Node 2 → Pods
  └── Node 3 → Pods

Control Plane (one-liners):
  API Server          → front door for kubectl / all components
  Scheduler           → picks which Node gets a new Pod
  Controller Manager  → keeps desired state (e.g. Deployment controllers)
  etcd                → cluster state database


═══════════════════════════════════════
Easy interview hierarchies
═══════════════════════════════════════

Structure:
  Cluster → Node → Pod → Container

App management:
  Deployment → ReplicaSet → Pods

Networking (external HTTP):
  Ingress → Service → Pods


